From: FunnyPlaying RetroPixel Pocket port
Subject: [PATCH] midgard: balance runtime PM clock ownership

The generic devicetree runtime-PM callbacks use __clk_is_enabled() to decide
whether Mali should acquire or release its clock. That helper reports the
global hardware gate state, not whether this consumer owns an enable
reference. On RK3326 the GPU clock can remain physically enabled through its
CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED child clocks after Mali releases its own reference. This
causes a warning after a correctly balanced disable and can make the next
power-on path skip acquiring Mali's reference.

power_control_init() already enables every clock once to match the first
runtime suspend. Keep ownership balanced after that: acquire one reference on
every Mali power-on callback and release one on every power-off callback.
This mirrors patch 003's regulator fix and avoids using global clock state as
consumer ownership state.

--- a/product/kernel/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/platform/devicetree/mali_kbase_runtime_pm.c
+++ b/product/kernel/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/platform/devicetree/mali_kbase_runtime_pm.c
@@ -46,7 +46,7 @@ static void enable_gpu_power_control(struct kbase_device *kbdev)
 	for (i = 0; i < kbdev->nr_clocks; i++) {
 		if (WARN_ON(kbdev->clocks[i] == NULL))
 			;
-		else if (!__clk_is_enabled(kbdev->clocks[i]))
+		else
 			WARN_ON(clk_prepare_enable(kbdev->clocks[i]));
 	}
 }
@@ -58,10 +58,8 @@ static void disable_gpu_power_control(struct kbase_device *kbdev)
 	for (i = 0; i < kbdev->nr_clocks; i++) {
 		if (WARN_ON(kbdev->clocks[i] == NULL))
 			;
-		else if (__clk_is_enabled(kbdev->clocks[i])) {
+		else
 			clk_disable_unprepare(kbdev->clocks[i]);
-			WARN_ON(__clk_is_enabled(kbdev->clocks[i]));
-		}
 	}
 
 #if defined(CONFIG_REGULATOR)
